WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.497
This query uses CTEs and a window function.

00:00:02.497 --> 00:00:05.492
The monthly sales stage has one row per month and product.

00:00:05.492 --> 00:00:09.037
The ranked stage keeps that grain and adds a rank within each month.

00:00:09.037 --> 00:00:11.842
The final SELECT keeps the top two rank groups.

00:00:11.842 --> 00:00:15.867
This is a good example of readable staged SQL that can transfer to Spark SQL.

00:00:15.867 --> 00:00:19.617
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:19.617 --> 00:00:24.892
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:24.892 --> 00:00:26.917
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:26.917 --> 00:00:31.657
In a real setting such as a lakehouse processing millions of web events across many files,

00:00:31.657 --> 00:00:37.082
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:37.082 --> 00:00:38.342
before discussing syntax.

00:00:38.342 --> 00:00:43.520
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
